			<div class="content" id="post-content-2053354">
									

<p>I was intrigued by the concept of a locked-room mystery where the room <em>itself</em> is the mystery. Something as simple as an office becoming inexplicably inaccessible was appealing and I was compelled to keep reading. When I came to the &quot;mutilated bodies&quot; bit, however, I was immediately snapped out of the reverie.</p>
<p>For me, the entire concept of the &quot;tenants&quot; is superfluous and somehow&#8230;tacky? This was concise and elegant before adding alternate universe Marlowe butchery, and its inclusion feels like a disservice to the delightful strangeness that preceded it. Null vote for me.</p>

